From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan: Fritchii's render-target relocation and VkImage pool

Ported from Frizti/Fritchii c98e63520 -> 07b38b3ca -> 4f68b8a0c -> 1735a6fd1, the four commits behind
v5's "**~2.4 GB less video memory** (8.8 to 6.4 GB in Castle Town)" and "about +3 fps in Castle Town
from each of the fixes behind it (render target relocation, image pool, ...)". Fritchii's own source
calls them EXP42 and EXP43.

Two mechanisms, and both target the same measured churn:

* **The VkImage pool.** A deleted render target normally destroys its VkImage and VMA allocation, and
  the next frame asks for one of the same shape. The pool intercepts only after `scheduler.IsFree()`
  says every GPU use is complete, then retains that exact image keyed by its full create identity
  (flags, format, extent, levels, layers, samples, usage, view-format table). A later matching
  single-level, single-layer, single-sample 2D attachment takes the object instead of calling
  `vkDestroyImage` + `vkCreateImage` + a new allocation. It is capped at 512 MiB and 180 frames, is
  emptied on a pressure event, and excludes sparse, unbacked, non-device-local, dedicated and sub-256
  KiB allocations. Taken images are treated as new - uninitialised, first transition discarded - so no
  stale contents are ever presented.
* **Render-target relocation.** When a new render target overlaps a predecessor badly, the predecessor
  is moved in place rather than retired: page tables, CPU/GPU addresses, allocation indexing, overlap
  links, bindings, descriptors and LRU are updated while its VkImage, memory, views and framebuffers
  stay. The clear that follows overwrites the new address, so nothing is uploaded. The candidate must
  match in shape and layout, drift by less than one image, have real CPU addresses, carry no queued
  download, aliases, remapping, sparse/converted/rescaled/unbacked/costly state, and not be another
  bound target; GPU-written predecessors that were sampled, rendered without a clear, or CPU-read
  sticky-guard the shape.

Why this port needs it: our log has 2633 images created and 3615 deleted in a single 600-frame window,
841 costly collector deletions, 669 downloads, a heap at 6274 of 6422 MiB (98%), and present frame time
going from 31 ms to 61 ms as the scene fills.

Escapes, both live from env.txt: `EDEN_NX2_EXP42` (1 = pool, the default; 0 = off) and
`EDEN_NX2_EXP43` (0 off, 1 retire immediately, 2 relocate only with exactly one predecessor,
3 = move in place, the default). Setting both to 0 returns the previous behaviour.

What this can break, in order of how likely it is to hurt:
* **A missed guard.** If a predecessor whose contents were still needed gets relocated, the image shows
  cleared or new-address contents: missing textures, smearing, flicker, a wrong frame - visible before
  it is fatal.
* **Bookkeeping drift.** Failing to unregister an old page entry, descriptor, overlap link or
  framebuffer makes two logical images share a slot or leaves a stale view. That shows as corrupted
  draws, an assertion, an invalid image id, or a GPU hang.
* **The pool fed too early.** Taking an image before the GPU is done with it is a use-after-submit, and
  turns a memory problem into a device loss or a host crash. The pool must only be fed from the
  GPU-complete path; this is the single most dangerous part of the change.
* **Pressure accounting.** Retained pool bytes still count against the driver. If the cache estimate
  subtracts them wrongly, or a pressure event is missed, the pool holds memory on a full heap and we get
  `VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY` and the unbacked/black images this round is trying to remove.

Measured or a guess: the churn counts, the heap percentage and the frame-time doubling are from our own
log. The ~2.4 GB and the fps figures are Fritchii's release notes, not reproduced here. The 643 added
/ 114 removed lines were hand-adapted against a diverged `texture_cache.h` and **have never been
compiled or run** - this is the largest and riskiest change in the round, and it goes in default-on
only because it carries its own kill switch.

